---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";

const resolver = `
type FormResolver<TSchema, T, TPath extends string = string> =
  (schema: TSchema) => FormAdapter<T, TPath>;

interface FormAdapter<T, TPath extends string = string> {
  readonly fields: readonly FormFieldDescriptor[];
  validate(root: unknown): MaybeAsync<readonly FormIssue[]>;
}
`;

const layers = [
  {
    n: "Layer 1",
    head: "<AutoForm />",
    body: "Draws every declared field, in declaration order. Nothing to write.",
  },
  {
    n: "Layer 2",
    head: '<Field path="…" as="postcode" />',
    body: "Keeps the layout and names a widget from a registry.",
  },
  {
    n: "Layer 3",
    head: "<Field>{(field) => …}</Field>",
    body: "Plain React. No second DSL, and no class name of ours.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="form-contract — one function turns a validator into a form"
  description="A contract that lets any validator describe itself as a form, and a React runtime that updates only the fields that moved. The benchmark publishes its own resolution before its results."
>
  <p class="text-sm font-medium tracking-wide text-primary">form-contract</p>
  <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-bold leading-tight sm:text-5xl">
    One function turns a validator into a form.
  </h1>

  <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    A form renderer needs to know what a field accepts <em>before</em> anyone
    types in it. A validator knows, and has no way to say so — validation
    answers whether a value is acceptable, and that answer arrives after the
    value exists. Too late to decide whether to draw a number input or a
    select, or what to put in <code>minlength</code>.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    This is the other half: a contract, and a form runtime built on it.
  </p>

  <Code code={resolver} />

  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    A resolver is a plain function, named at the call site. No registry, no
    vendor tag, no dispatch — whoever writes the call already knows which
    validator they are using. <a
      class="text-primary underline underline-offset-4"
      href="/contract/">The contract in full →</a
    >
  </p>

  <!-- ================================================== layers -->
  <section class="mt-20">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Three heights, one primitive</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
      Schema-driven form libraries usually buy their convenience by taking the
      markup away. Here the generated screen is the top layer rather than the
      only one, and there is no second implementation for the heights to
      diverge between.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
      {
        layers.map((layer) => (
          <div class="rounded-lg bg-surface-low p-5">
            <p class="text-xs font-medium tracking-wide text-primary">{layer.n}</p>
            <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-sm break-words">{layer.head}</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
              {layer.body}
            </p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
      <strong class="text-on-surface">Mounting is a subscription and nothing
      else.</strong> Every cell exists from <code>createForm</code>, seeded from
      the descriptors, so a component never registers a field, seeds a default
      or resets anything on unmount. A field in a portal, in a lazily loaded
      chunk or behind a condition is therefore not a case: there is no{" "}
      <code>shouldUnregister</code> and no inspection of the React tree anywhere
      in the runtime.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================== cross-field -->
  <section class="mt-20">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">A rule can report against a field nobody touched</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
      One whole-root pass per settled change, scattered back into per-path issue
      cells, writing only where the content actually differs. So a cross-field
      rule lands its error on the <em>other</em> path with no{" "}
      <code>deps</code> array and no dependents list — and the same computation,
      filtered to one path, is what <code>form.field(path).validate()</code>{" "}
      runs, so a conditionally rendered field cannot get a different verdict
      from a second engine.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
      The cost is stated rather than hidden: validation is proportional to the
      schema on every settled change. The diff keeps <em>notification</em>{" "}
      proportional to what moved; the pass itself judges the whole root.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================== measured -->
  <section class="mt-20">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Measured, with the losses printed first</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
      Five runtimes are driven through one scripted transcript against one zod
      schema the harness owns and instruments, rendering one DOM the harness
      owns and hashes. Every count is an integer that does not depend on the
      machine, and CI fails when one of them moves.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
      On the deterministic lane form-contract is <em>not</em> first, and the
      report sorts the rows it loses first. It does marginally more React work
      per keystroke than the hand-written reference it is measured against. That
      table is published here as it comes out of the file CI gates against.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6">
      <a
        class="inline-block rounded-xl bg-primary px-5 py-3 text-sm font-medium text-on-primary"
        href="/benchmark/">Read what the benchmark can and cannot see →</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================== status -->
  <section class="mt-20 rounded-lg border border-outline-variant p-6">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">Where it stands</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
      The runtime is complete against its design and is exercised by 87 tests, a
      compile-time test that pins the path union, and a screen that uses all of
      it. <strong class="text-on-surface"
        >It has not been published to npm, and it has not been run in production
        by anyone.</strong
      >
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
      One more limit, stated rather than papered over: the concrete path grammar
      has no escape, so a field whose key contains a dot cannot be addressed.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
